---
title: "A complete analysis workflow"
output:
  rmarkdown::html_vignette:
vignette: >
  %\VignetteIndexEntry{A complete analysis workflow}
  %\VignetteEngine{knitr::rmarkdown}
  %\VignetteEncoding{UTF-8}
bibliography: bayesnec.bib
---

```{r setup, include = FALSE}
stopifnot(require(knitr))
# seed = in bnec() governs Stan's sampler only. The initial values bayesnec
# generates are drawn in R, so a set.seed() here is required as well before the
# vignette is reproducible. See vignette("example1").
set.seed(17)
# Printed output wraps at getOption("width") before it reaches the page, so a
# wide table breaks across lines whatever the rendered page does. 100 fits
# summary()'s weights table and check_fit()'s columns on one line each.
options(width = 100)
knitr::opts_chunk$set(
  collapse = TRUE,
  comment = "#>",
  warning = FALSE,
  message = FALSE,
  fig.path = "vignette-fig-",
  fig.asp = 0.8,
  out.width = "60%",
  fig.align = "center",
  dev = "png",
  dpi = 150,
  eval = identical(Sys.getenv("NOT_CRAN"), "true")
)
# In a chunk with drop_progress = TRUE, leave out every message line from the
# first "Finding initial values" up to "Fitted models are": the per-equation
# progress of bnec() and the sampler's own warnings.
knitr::knit_hooks$set(message = local({
  default_hook <- knitr::knit_hooks$get("message")
  dropping <- FALSE
  function(x, options) {
    if (!isTRUE(options$drop_progress)) return(default_hook(x, options))
    lines <- strsplit(x, "\n", fixed = TRUE)[[1]]
    keep <- logical(length(lines))
    for (i in seq_along(lines)) {
      if (grepl("Finding initial values", lines[i], fixed = TRUE)) dropping <<- TRUE
      if (grepl("Fitted models are", lines[i], fixed = TRUE)) dropping <<- FALSE
      keep[i] <- !dropping
    }
    if (!any(keep)) return("")
    default_hook(paste0(paste(lines[keep], collapse = "\n"), "\n"), options)
  }
}))
# Helpers for inline text only. They keep a sentence grammatical whatever a
# later render returns, including an empty set of equations.
name_list <- function(x, empty = "none") {
  if (!length(x)) return(empty)
  x <- paste0("`", x, "`")
  if (length(x) == 1) x else
    paste(paste(x[-length(x)], collapse = ", "), "and", x[length(x)])
}
range_text <- function(x, fmt = "%.2f") {
  r <- sprintf(fmt, range(x))
  if (r[1] == r[2]) r[1] else paste(r[1], "to", r[2])
}
which_failed <- function(fit) {
  f <- names(failed_models(fit))
  if (length(f)) name_list(f) else "no equation"
}
```

# Overview

The other vignettes each explain one part of `bayesnec` (e.g., what the model
set is, what a `bayesmanecfit` is, how priors are built, and how to handle a
two-block response). Here we run one analysis, on one plate of a bioluminescence
toxicity test, from the data through to a reportable estimate, taking the steps
in the order in which they occur:

1. Prepare the data, and choose the family and the terms the design requires.
2. Fit the candidate set under the default priors, and read its summary.
3. Check the sampler, refit the equations whose failure has a remedy, and screen
   out those that still fail.
4. Refit the set under regularising priors, check the sampler again, and choose
   the prior set used for inference.
5. Check the fit of the retained equations, and refit with a dispersion
   sub-model where the spread is not reproduced.
6. Assess the influence of the priors, and report.

The construction of the default priors, and `check_priors()`, are described in
the [*Priors*](https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example3.html)
vignette.

## The assumptions the diagnostics test

A Bayesian fit is usually judged on convergence, although convergence is not the
only thing that can be wrong with it. `rhat()`, `check_chains()` and
`check_sampling()` ask whether the sampler explored the posterior, and `loo` and
`waic` rank the candidate equations against one another. However, none of them
asks whether the variability implied by the fitted model matches the variability
in the data. That assumption can affect a threshold estimate even when the
sampling diagnostics are satisfactory.

The *NSEC* depends on it most directly. `nsec()` sets its reference at a
quantile of the posterior of the control mean, and the width of that posterior
depends in part on the dispersion estimated at the control. If a model simulates
more variability at the control than the data show, the reference is placed
lower, so the curve crosses it further along and the reported concentration is
higher and less protective. If a model simulates less variability, the reference
is placed higher and a lower concentration is returned. The direction of the
error is not known in advance, and no convergence diagnostic reveals it (this
point is also made, for the choice between constant and non-constant dispersion,
in [*Single model
usage*](https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example1.html)). The ECx
depends on this assumption less. Although it is also measured from the control
mean, it is measured at a fixed fraction of that mean and not at a quantile of
its posterior, so an error in the variance widens or narrows the ECx interval
without relocating the reference it is measured from.

A single ratio statistic for the whole fit does not detect this kind of error.
Each family with a free dispersion parameter (i.e., `gaussian`, `Gamma`,
`negbinomial`, `Beta` and `beta_binomial`) estimates that parameter from the
residuals that such a statistic summarises. The parameter therefore absorbs the
discrepancy, so that the ratio is near 1 while the fit simulates the wrong
spread at the concentrations that determine the estimate. For this reason the
check is made within concentration groups, which is what `check_fit()` does.

## The effect of exclusion

[*Multi model
usage*](https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example2.html) notes that
a poorly shaped model with a low predictive weight may contribute little to a
model average. A model that has not sampled adequately, however, contributes on
the basis of a posterior the sampler has not explored reliably. Because its
weight is computed from that same posterior, the weight provides no evidence
that it is safe to keep. Removing such a model changes the candidate set, and
`amend()` then recomputes the weights across the models that remain (it does not
rescale the weights of those retained), and the reported *N(S)EC* changes
accordingly.

Which models a screen will remove cannot be predicted before it is run, and the
reasons for removal differ between them. An equation given too few draws, an
equation whose posterior the sampler cannot traverse, and an equation the design
does not support are all reported under one failure flag, although the
appropriate response to each is different.

## The case study

The analysis uses one plate of the copper series of the `lum31`
bioluminescence assay, read at 15 minutes. It ends with a draft of the methods
and results text a paper might give for it, in which steps 1 to 6 are presented
as the supplementary material. The threshold estimates themselves (*NEC*,
*NSEC*, ECx, and the model-averaged *N(S)EC* reported when a set mixes both
kinds) are defined, together with the candidate equations, in [*Model
details*](https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example2b.html).

```{r libraries}
library(bayesnec)
library(ggplot2)
```

# 1. Data, design and family

The `lum31` data contain the results of acute copper and zinc toxicity tests run
on the Lum-31 bioluminescent bacterial assay [@luter2025]. A suspension of
marine bacteria is dosed in a microplate well and its light output is read after
a fixed exposure, so the response is luminescence in relative light units
(`?lum31` documents the columns).

We use one plate of the copper series read at 15 minutes (plate
`Cu 19Mar24 Rep1B`). A plate is a complete test (i.e., eleven concentrations by
four replicate wells). Because the plate reader sets its gain for each read,
plates are not on a common vertical scale, and taking a single plate avoids that
difference instead of modelling it. The group-level terms that handle the whole
assay are described in [*Grouping and factor
covariates*](https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example8.html).

```{r lum31-data}
data(lum31)
cu <- droplevels(subset(lum31, plate == "Cu 19Mar24 Rep1B" & minutes == 15))
c(wells = nrow(cu), concentrations = length(unique(cu$conc)),
  at_the_floor = sum(cu$censoring != "none"))
```

```{r copper-design-text, include = FALSE}
conc_levels <- sort(unique(cu$conc))
conc_means <- tapply(cu$rlu_cens, cu$conc, mean)
mean_share <- conc_means / conc_means[1]
last_high <- max(which(mean_share > 0.9))
first_low <- min(which(mean_share < 0.01))
cens_conc <- unique(cu$conc[cu$censoring != "none"])
# TRUE for each concentration with no censored reading. A tolerance,
# because a group label is a printed concentration and not the stored double.
uncensored <- function(group) {
  vapply(as.numeric(as.character(group)),
         function(v) !any(abs(v - cens_conc) < 1e-8), logical(1))
}
```

This plate is one of two in the arm of
`r length(unique(subset(lum31, toxicant == "Cu" & minutes == 15)$plate))` with
the fewest readings at the floor of the instrument (one of `r nrow(cu)`). A
reading held at a bound contributes a smaller spread than the well would have
shown, so a plate with many such readings would understate the spread examined
in step 5.

```{r exmp9-copper-data, fig.asp = 0.72, out.width = "80%"}
copper_axis <- list(
  scale_x_continuous(transform = "log", breaks = c(0.003, 0.03, 0.3, 3),
                     labels = function(x) signif(x, 2)),
  scale_y_continuous(labels = function(x) {
    format(x, big.mark = ",", scientific = FALSE)
  }),
  labs(x = expression(paste("Copper (mg ", L^-1, ")")),
       y = "Relative light units")
)
ggplot(cu, aes(x = conc, y = rlu_cens)) +
  geom_point(aes(shape = censoring), alpha = 0.7, size = 2) +
  copper_axis +
  theme_classic()
```

Each point is one well, and the reading at the floor is drawn with its own
symbol. The mean response remains above
`r round(100 * mean_share[last_high])` per cent of its control value up to
`r signif(conc_levels[last_high], 2)` mg/L, and is below one per cent from
`r signif(conc_levels[first_low], 2)` mg/L, so the whole decline occurs over a
factor of about `r round(conc_levels[first_low] / conc_levels[last_high])` in
concentration. Over the highest `r sum(mean_share < 0.01)` concentrations the
response stays near the floor.

The four wells at a concentration share one cell of the plate, and readings from
one cell can vary together. Each fit below therefore includes `ogl(conc_group)`,
a deviation for each cell, so that the cell (and not the well) is the
observation at each concentration ([*Grouping and factor
covariates*](https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example8.html)
describes the term and how the model weights are computed under it). The
dispersion parameter then describes the spread of the wells within a cell.

Concentrations are measured dissolved copper, and the lowest
(`r conc_levels[1]` mg/L) serves as the control, because `bayesnec` takes the
control to be the lowest value of the predictor. The concentrations span
`r sprintf("%.1f", log10(max(conc_levels) / min(conc_levels)))` orders of
magnitude, so we fit copper on a log scale, and because the lowest value is
positive no substitution is needed before taking logarithms.

Luminescence is continuous and positive, so we use a `Gamma` family with an
identity link, which keeps `top` and any estimated lower asymptote (`bot`) on
the scale of relative light units. Readings were blank-corrected, and a well
emitting less light than the blank was recorded as zero. Such a reading is
below the floor of the instrument and is not an absence of light, so it is
left-censored: `rlu_cens | cens(censoring)` replaces it by the smallest positive
reading on the plate and declares that the true value is below that bound
(`?lum31`, and *Censoring* in [*Single model
usage*](https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example1.html)). On this
plate `r sum(cu$censoring != "none")` reading is censored, at
`r signif(cens_conc, 2)` mg/L. A `Gamma` family with one `shape` parameter
assumes a constant coefficient of variation (i.e., the standard deviation of a
well is a fixed multiple of the fitted mean at each concentration), and step 5
checks that assumption.

# 2. The candidate set and its priors

The copper response falls from its control, so we request `model = "decline"`,
which includes the constant equation `ecxflat` (a response independent of
concentration) and the equations for a declining response. The zinc series in
this dataset rises above its control at low concentrations, which is why `all`
is fitted to it in *Grouping and factor covariates*. An equation that fits or
samples poorly is not made suitable by being assigned a small model weight, and
steps 3 to 5 check each one.

Some equations in `decline` estimate a lower asymptote (`bot`), whereas the
others decline towards zero. Here the response stays near the floor over the
highest concentrations (step 1), so the design includes the lower limb from
which `bot` is estimated.

The family and the logged predictor also rule out equations before sampling.
Under an identity link `neclin` and `ecxlin` can predict negative means, which a
`Gamma` family cannot take, and `ecxsigm` cannot evaluate its fractional power
at the negative logged concentrations. `get_priors()` returns entries only for
the equations that remain valid ([*Model
details*](https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example2b.html) gives
their equations, and `?models` the model groups).

```{r copper-priors}
gfam <- Gamma(link = "identity")
copper_formula <- rlu_cens | cens(censoring) ~
  crf(log(conc), model = "decline") + ogl(conc_group)
prior_default <- get_priors(copper_formula, data = cu, family = gfam,
                            prior_type = "uninformative")
names(prior_default)
```

We fit these `r length(prior_default)` equations first under the default
`"uninformative"` priors (`?bnec` describes the set). Step 4 fits them again
under the `"regularizing"` priors, which provide the sampler with more
information about plausible parameter values, to test whether that information
allows the equations that fail in step 3 to be sampled. For example, the default
entries for the `top`, `bot`, `ec50` and `f` parameters of `ecxll5` are:

```{r copper-prior-entries}
selected_priors <- function(x) {
  p <- as.data.frame(x[["ecxll5"]])
  p <- p[p$nlpar %in% c("top", "bot", "ec50", "f"),
         c("nlpar", "prior", "lb", "ub")]
  rownames(p) <- NULL
  p
}
entries_default <- selected_priors(prior_default)
entries_default
```

`top` and `bot` are gamma priors on the response scale. `ec50` is a normal prior
on log concentration, bounded below by the logged control and (because
`asymptote_observed = TRUE`, the default) above by the logged highest
concentration. `f`, the exponent that sets the asymmetry of `ecxll5`, is
`r entries_default$prior[entries_default$nlpar == "f"]`.
[*Priors*](https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example3.html) explains
how the entries are constructed and how to compare them with the posterior.

## Fitting the full set

`bnec()` uses four chains and 10,000 iterations per chain by default. Under its
warmup rule the first 8,000 iterations of each chain are discarded, so 8,000
post-warmup draws remain across the four chains. We use these defaults here and
for the refits in steps 4 and 5, with identical data and seed. The `seed`
argument controls Stan's sampler, whereas `set.seed()` controls the initial
values `bayesnec` generates in R, so both are set. The precompiled vignette uses
the `cmdstanr` backend
(through `options(brms.backend = "cmdstanr")`); without that option `brms` uses
`rstan`, and the diagnostic counts below may differ.

```{r copper-fit-default, results = "hide", message = TRUE, drop_progress = TRUE}
set.seed(17)
fit_default <- bnec(copper_formula, data = cu, family = gfam,
                    prior_type = "uninformative", seed = 17, refresh = 0)
```

While fitting, `bnec()` also prints a line as it begins and completes each
equation, and the sampler prints its own warnings, including the number of
divergent transitions; those lines are omitted above, and step 3 examines the
same diagnostics with `check_sampling()`. Before fitting, `bnec()` names the
equations it drops, for the reasons given above. The messages after fitting
concern the fitted equations. They report, where any occur, the draws for which
a curve does not fall to the NSEC reference within the tested range (those
draws are censored in the NSEC summary), the cells whose Pareto *k* is at or
above 0.7 (step 3), and the equations whose control mean or spread is not
reproduced within 15% (step 5).

```{r copper-record}
record_default <- bnec_record(fit_default)
record_default$excluded
is.null(record_default$substitutions)
```

`bnec_record()` gives the equations excluded before fitting, with the reasons
above, and any value changed to make the response compatible with the family
(`substitutions`, which is `NULL` here because `rlu_cens` is strictly positive).
Every requested equation is either excluded or `attempted`, and an attempted
equation that did not fit remains in `attempted`. `failed_models()` lists such
equations, and in this run `r which_failed(fit_default)` failed to fit. It also
keeps the priors and initial values used for each failed equation, which are
useful when investigating the failure. A different initialisation or prior may
allow such a fit to start (step 4 refits the set under other priors), although
that does not show that the resulting posterior is identified by these
concentrations.

## The summary of the fitted set

`summary()` gives the model weights, computed from leave-one-cell-out scores,
the model-averaged *N(S)EC*, and the Bayesian R² and number of divergent
transitions of each equation. It ends with a warning that names each equation
whose maximum Rhat exceeds 1.01 or which has more than ten divergent
transitions, and each equation whose control mean or spread is not reproduced
within 15%.

```{r copper-summary}
summary_default <- summary(fit_default)
```

```{r copper-summary-print, warning = TRUE}
summary_default
```

```{r copper-summary-text, include = FALSE}
flag_default <- with(check_sampling(fit_default),
                     model[which(max_rhat > 1.01 | n_divergent > 10)])
flag_weight <- sum(summary_default$mod_weights[flag_default, "wi"])
flag_text <- if (!length(flag_default)) {
  paste("In this run no equation exceeds the Rhat or divergence cutoff,",
        "although step 3 also applies an ESS cutoff that the summary does not",
        "report.")
} else {
  paste0("In this run the warning names ", length(flag_default), " of the ",
         length(fit_default$success_models), " fitted equations for their ",
         "Rhat or divergent transitions, and together they hold ",
         sprintf("%.2f", flag_weight), " of the model weight. The ",
         "model-averaged *N(S)EC* in the summary therefore includes equations ",
         "whose posteriors the sampler may not have explored reliably, and it ",
         "is not an estimate to report.")
}
```

These weights are computed over every fitted equation, including any that the
warning names. `r flag_text` Step 3 examines the sampler diagnostics of each
equation and removes those that fail them before any estimate is read.

# 3. Sampler diagnostics and screening

An attempted equation that produces draws must still be checked. The package
screen uses a maximum Rhat of 1.01, a minimum effective sample size (ESS, the
smaller of the bulk and tail values) of 400, and at most ten divergent
transitions. The Rhat and ESS cutoffs follow the recommendations of
@Vehtari2021, whereas the allowance of ten divergences is a `bayesnec`
convention (Stan regards any divergence as evidence that some part of the
posterior was not explored). A screen result is specific to the data, priors,
sampler settings and seed. The table gives the curve parameters of each valid
equation, its maximum Rhat, minimum ESS and number of divergent transitions
under the default priors, and whether it failed the screen.

```{r copper-sampling}
round_cols <- function(x, digits = 3) {
  num <- vapply(x, is.numeric, logical(1))
  x[num] <- lapply(x[num], round, digits)
  x
}
sampling_default <- check_sampling(fit_default)
valid <- names(prior_default)
curve_parameters <- lapply(valid, function(m) names(show_params(m)[[1]]$pforms))
first_run <- sampling_default[match(valid, sampling_default$model), ]
data.frame(
  model = valid,
  parameters = vapply(curve_parameters, paste, character(1), collapse = ", "),
  max_rhat = round(first_run$max_rhat, 3),
  min_ess = round(first_run$min_ess),
  n_divergent = first_run$n_divergent,
  failed = first_run$failed,
  row.names = NULL
)
```

```{r copper-sampling-counts, include = FALSE}
passed <- function(tab) tab$model[!tab$failed]
bot_models <- valid[vapply(curve_parameters, function(p) "bot" %in% p,
                           logical(1))]
n_divergence_fail <- function(tab) sum(tab$failed & tab$n_divergent > 10)
```

A row of `NA` marks an attempted equation with no draws, which is distinct from
a fitted equation that missed a cutoff. Under the default priors
`r length(passed(sampling_default))` of the `r nrow(sampling_default)` fitted
equations passed (`r name_list(passed(sampling_default))`), and
`r n_divergence_fail(sampling_default)` of the
`r sum(sampling_default$failed)` that failed had more than ten divergent
transitions. Of the `r length(bot_models)` equations with a `bot` parameter,
`r sum(bot_models %in% passed(sampling_default))` passed, against
`r sum(setdiff(valid, bot_models) %in% passed(sampling_default))` of the
`r length(valid) - length(bot_models)` equations without one. The diagnostics do
not show which parameter is responsible for a failure.

## Kinds of failure

The `failed` flag does not distinguish which cutoff was missed, although the
remedy differs between them. An equation that misses the Rhat or ESS cutoff with
few divergences may only have been given too few draws, and more iterations may
raise its ESS. Divergences describe a different problem: additional draws visit
the difficult part of the posterior more often, so increasing `iter` by itself
is unlikely to remove them. A higher `adapt_delta` (set through the `control`
argument) makes the sampler take smaller steps and may reduce them. If Stan also
warns about maximum tree depth, `max_treedepth` is the relevant control to
examine (see `?check_sampling`). An equation that never initialises is recorded
by `failed_models()`, and has no sampler diagnostics to screen.

The ESS floor and the divergence allowance are both absolute counts. At an
unchanged divergence rate a longer run will record more divergences, while it
may also provide enough draws for an ESS above 400. A comparison between runs
therefore needs their chain counts, iterations and warmup beside the diagnostic
table. The decision also uses `min_ess` (an absolute count) and not
`min_ess_ratio` (a measure of sampling efficiency), so a model can meet the
count after many draws while still sampling inefficiently; `check_sampling()`
returns both. The 1.01 Rhat cutoff is also the one used by `rhat()` and
`summary()`.

An equation that fails on Rhat or ESS with no more than ten divergent
transitions should be refitted under the same prior with more iterations before
it is excluded, because changing the prior would not test whether the original
run was too short. The chunk below refits any such equation with twice the
iterations and warmup, thinned by two so that it keeps the 8,000 draws of the
other equations (the model weights compare leave-out scores computed from equal
numbers of draws), and replaces it in the set before screening. An equation
with more than ten divergent transitions is instead refitted with the same
iterations at `adapt_delta = 0.99`, or at 0.999 if its first fit already used
0.99. Steps 4 and 5 apply the same rules to their refits.

```{r copper-retry, results = "hide"}
retry_models <- function(tab) tab$model[tab$failed & tab$n_divergent <= 10]
tune_models <- function(tab) tab$model[tab$failed & tab$n_divergent > 10]
refit_failed <- function(fit, retry, tune) {
  if (!length(retry) && !length(tune)) return(fit)
  longer <- lapply(retry, function(m) {
    update(pull_out(fit, model = m), iter = 2e4, warmup = 1.6e4, thin = 2,
           refresh = 0)
  })
  tuned <- lapply(tune, function(m) {
    first <- brms::control_params(pull_brmsfit(fit, model = m))$adapt_delta
    delta <- if (isTRUE(first >= 0.99)) 0.999 else 0.99
    update(pull_out(fit, model = m), control = list(adapt_delta = delta),
           refresh = 0)
  })
  # c() keeps the first copy of an equation it is given twice, so the refits
  # come first.
  do.call(c, c(longer, tuned, list(fit)))
}
retry_default <- retry_models(sampling_default)
tune_default <- tune_models(sampling_default)
set.seed(17)
refit_default <- refit_failed(fit_default, retry_default, tune_default)
sampling_default_refit <- check_sampling(refit_default)
```

```{r copper-retried}
refitted_default <- c(retry_default, tune_default)
if (length(refitted_default)) {
  round_cols(sampling_default_refit[sampling_default_refit$model %in%
                                      refitted_default, ])
}
```

In this run the longer refit was applied to
`r name_list(retry_default, "no equation")`, and the refit at a higher
`adapt_delta` to `r name_list(tune_default, "no equation")`.
`r if (length(refitted_default)) "The table above gives the diagnostics of each refitted equation after its refit, and the screen below uses those." else "No equation was refitted, so no table is shown."`

## Screening the model set

`screen_models()` drops each equation that misses any cutoff, names it with the
cutoffs it failed, and recomputes the model weights over the equations that
pass. Its messages are the record a methods section should cite, so we leave
them visible. The constant equation `ecxflat` may remain because it samples
well, even where it has almost no predictive weight: the screen asks whether the
posterior draws are reliable, and does not select equations for their fit to the
observed response. If every fitted equation fails, the function stops with the
reasons instead of returning a model average.

```{r copper-screen, message = TRUE}
screened_default <- screen_models(refit_default)
```

After the refits, the screen retained
`r length(screened_default$success_models)` of the
`r nrow(sampling_default)` fitted equations
(`r name_list(screened_default$success_models)`).

## Cell-level model weights

The four wells in a cell are related observations, so leaving out one well at a
time would overstate how well a model predicts a new cell. Because
`ogl(conc_group)` is in the formula, the default model weights instead leave out
a whole cell at a time, and are computed from those leave-one-cell-out scores by
the default pseudo-BMA method. A cell here is a whole concentration, so leaving
it out removes a point on the curve. Pareto *k* assesses whether the
importance-sampling approximation to each left-out score is reliable, and the
score of a cell with *k* at or above 0.7 is unreliable until an exact refit
replaces it. `check_fit()` returns these diagnostics for each equation as its
`"pareto"` attribute, and we show the model weight, the number of cells at or
above 0.7 and the largest *k* for each retained equation. This check belongs
after screening, because the weights of models whose posterior was not sampled
adequately should not be interpreted.

```{r copper-pareto}
pareto_rows <- function(x) {
  p <- attr(check_fit(x, ndraws = 100), "pareto")
  if (anyNA(p$n_high_k)) stop("Pareto diagnostics are unavailable")
  p[, c("model", "weight", "n_high_k", "max_k")]
}
pareto_default <- pareto_rows(screened_default)
round_cols(pareto_default)
```

In this run `r sum(pareto_default$n_high_k > 0)` of the
`r nrow(pareto_default)` retained equations had at least one cell with *k* at or
above 0.7 (the largest *k* was
`r sprintf("%.2f", max(pareto_default$max_k))`).
`amend(x, loo_controls = list(refit = TRUE))` replaces each such score by
refitting the equation without that cell, which takes one refit for each cell at
or above the cutoff. The weights of this set are used only to compare it with
later fits, so we make the exact refits only for the set the reported estimates
come from (step 5), and the weights below are approximate.

```{r copper-weights}
weights_of <- function(x, label) {
  w <- summary(x)$mod_weights
  setNames(data.frame(rownames(w), w[, "wi"]), c("model", label))
}
weights_default <- weights_of(screened_default, "uninformative")
round_cols(weights_default)
```

```{r copper-weights-lead, include = FALSE}
lead_default <- weights_default$model[which.max(weights_default$uninformative)]
```

The weights are the share of the model-averaged prediction assigned to each
equation, and sum to one. `r name_list(lead_default)` has the highest weight
(`r sprintf("%.2f", max(weights_default$uninformative))`). A small weight does
not validate an equation that sampled poorly (e.g., an equation with many
divergent transitions can still be given a large weight), which is why the
weights are read only after screening.

## Chain mixing

The screen summarises each chain in a few numbers, whereas the traces show the
draws themselves. Here we inspect the traces for the highest-weighted equation:

```{r exmp9-chains, out.width = "90%"}
best_default <- pull_best(screened_default)
check_chains(best_default)
```

```{r copper-chains-text, include = FALSE}
best_row <- sampling_default_refit[sampling_default_refit$model ==
                                     best_default$model, ]
```

For `r name_list(best_default$model)` (maximum Rhat
`r sprintf("%.3f", best_row$max_rhat)`, minimum ESS
`r sprintf("%.0f", best_row$min_ess)`) the four chains overlap throughout a
stable range in the trace panels, and in the autocorrelation panels the
dependence between successive draws falls close to zero within a few lags. A
satisfactory Rhat does not replace inspection of these plots, and before
reporting a model average we would inspect the traces of each retained equation.
`check_chains()` can write one page per equation to a PDF for that review:

```{r copper-all-chains, eval = FALSE}
check_chains(screened_default, filename = "copper_chains")
```

# 4. The regularising priors

```{r copper-remaining, include = FALSE}
still_failed <- sampling_default_refit$model[sampling_default_refit$failed]
unfit_default <- setdiff(valid, fit_default$success_models)
remaining_text <- if (length(still_failed) || length(unfit_default)) {
  paste0("After the refits of step 3, ", length(still_failed), " of the ",
         nrow(sampling_default_refit), " fitted equations still failed the ",
         "screen under the default priors",
         if (length(still_failed)) paste0(" (", name_list(still_failed), ")"),
         if (length(unfit_default)) {
           paste0(", and ", name_list(unfit_default), " had not fitted at all")
         }, ".")
} else {
  paste("After the refits of step 3, every equation fitted and passed the",
        "screen under the default priors, so here the regularising priors are",
        "fitted only for comparison.")
}
```

`r remaining_text` The `"regularizing"` priors (`?bnec`) are narrower than the
defaults, and they centre `top` and `bot` on the mean response at the lowest
and the highest concentrations. They therefore provide the sampler with more
information about plausible parameter values, and refitting under them tests
whether the equations that failed can be fitted and sampled when the priors
provide that information. The entries for `ecxll5` that correspond to those of
step 2 are:

```{r copper-prior-regular}
prior_regular <- get_priors(copper_formula, data = cu, family = gfam,
                            prior_type = "regularizing")
entries_regular <- selected_priors(prior_regular)
entries_regular
```

The bounds of `ec50` are unchanged and its standard deviation is smaller, `top`
and `bot` are concentrated around the mean response at the lowest and the
highest concentrations, and `f` is
`r entries_regular$prior[entries_regular$nlpar == "f"]` instead of
`r entries_default$prior[entries_default$nlpar == "f"]`. Narrower priors may
stabilise sampling on sparse data, but they can also narrow the reported
interval where the likelihood provides little information.

We refit the whole candidate set under these priors, and not only the equations
that failed. Step 6 compares the estimates of the equations that pass under both
prior sets, which requires each of them to be fitted under both. The model
weights of a set that combined equations fitted under different priors would
depend on those priors as well as on the shapes of the curves, so such a set
could not be reported as fitted under one prior set.

```{r copper-fit-regular, results = "hide"}
set.seed(17)
fit_regular <- bnec(copper_formula, data = cu, family = gfam,
                    prior_type = "regularizing", seed = 17, refresh = 0)
```

```{r copper-rescued, include = FALSE}
rescued <- setdiff(fit_regular$success_models, fit_default$success_models)
rescue_text <- if (length(rescued)) {
  paste0(" ", name_list(rescued), if (length(rescued) == 1) " was" else " were",
         " fitted under the regularising priors although ",
         if (length(rescued) == 1) "it" else "they",
         " failed to fit under the default priors. The refits of step 3 do not",
         " apply to such a failure, because an equation with no draws has no",
         " diagnostics to act on.")
} else {
  ""
}
```

This fit prints the same kinds of message as the fit of step 2, and they are not
shown. In this run `r which_failed(fit_regular)` failed to fit under the
regularising priors.`r rescue_text`

## Sampler diagnostics under both prior sets

The table compares the first run under each prior set, before any refit. Each
entry gives the maximum Rhat, the minimum ESS and the number of divergent
transitions, followed by the screen result, and "did not fit" marks an attempted
equation with no draws.

```{r copper-sampling-compare}
sampling_regular <- check_sampling(fit_regular)
diagnostic_label <- function(tab, model) {
  i <- match(model, tab$model)
  if (is.na(i)) return("did not fit")
  x <- tab[i, ]
  sprintf("%.3f / %.0f / %d %s", x$max_rhat, x$min_ess, x$n_divergent,
          if (x$failed) "fail" else "pass")
}
data.frame(
  model = valid,
  uninformative = vapply(valid, diagnostic_label, character(1),
                         tab = sampling_default),
  regularizing = vapply(valid, diagnostic_label, character(1),
                        tab = sampling_regular),
  row.names = NULL
)
```

```{r copper-sampling-compare-text, include = FALSE}
both_failed <- intersect(sampling_default$model[sampling_default$failed],
                         sampling_regular$model[sampling_regular$failed])
fewer_divergences <- sum(
  sampling_regular$n_divergent[match(both_failed, sampling_regular$model)] <
    sampling_default$n_divergent[match(both_failed, sampling_default$model)]
)
only_regular_first <- setdiff(passed(sampling_regular), passed(sampling_default))
only_default_first <- setdiff(passed(sampling_default), passed(sampling_regular))
first_run_text <- if (!length(only_regular_first) && !length(only_default_first)) {
  ", and the same equations passed under both"
} else {
  paste0(", and ", paste(c(
    if (length(only_regular_first)) {
      paste(name_list(only_regular_first),
            "passed only under the regularising priors")
    },
    if (length(only_default_first)) {
      paste(name_list(only_default_first),
            "passed only under the default priors")
    }), collapse = " whereas "))
}
```

On the first run `r length(passed(sampling_regular))` of the
`r nrow(sampling_regular)` fitted equations passed under the regularising
priors, against `r length(passed(sampling_default))` of
`r nrow(sampling_default)` under the default priors`r first_run_text`. Of the
`r length(both_failed)` equations that failed under both prior sets,
`r fewer_divergences` had fewer divergent transitions under the regularising
priors.

Stan also records the time each chain spent in warmup and in sampling, and
`rstan::get_elapsed_time()` reads it from the `stanfit` held in each equation's
`brmsfit`. The table gives the sum over the four chains, in seconds, for the
same first run. These times exclude compiling each Stan program and searching
for initial values, and they depend on the machine and on what else it was
running, so they are given for comparison between equations and prior sets
only.

```{r copper-sampling-time}
sampling_seconds <- function(fit) {
  vapply(valid, function(m) {
    if (!m %in% fit$success_models) return(NA_real_)
    sum(rstan::get_elapsed_time(pull_brmsfit(fit, model = m)$fit))
  }, numeric(1))
}
seconds_default <- sampling_seconds(fit_default)
seconds_regular <- sampling_seconds(fit_regular)
data.frame(model = valid, uninformative = round(seconds_default),
           regularizing = round(seconds_regular), row.names = NULL)
```

```{r copper-sampling-time-text, include = FALSE}
total_minutes <- function(s) signif(sum(s, na.rm = TRUE) / 60, 2)
failed_share <- function(s, tab) {
  failed <- names(s) %in% tab$model[tab$failed]
  sprintf("%.0f", 100 * sum(s[failed], na.rm = TRUE) / sum(s, na.rm = TRUE))
}
```

Warmup and sampling took `r total_minutes(seconds_default)` min in total under
the default priors and `r total_minutes(seconds_regular)` min under the
regularising priors, and `r failed_share(seconds_default, sampling_default)`%
and `r failed_share(seconds_regular, sampling_regular)`% of those totals were
spent on the equations that failed the screen.

## Refits and screening under the regularising priors

The equations that failed under the regularising priors are refitted by the
rules of step 3, and the set is then screened, and its cell-level weights
checked, in the same way.

```{r copper-retry-regular, results = "hide"}
retry_regular <- retry_models(sampling_regular)
tune_regular <- tune_models(sampling_regular)
set.seed(17)
refit_regular <- refit_failed(fit_regular, retry_regular, tune_regular)
sampling_regular_refit <- check_sampling(refit_regular)
```

```{r copper-screen-regular, message = TRUE}
screened_regular <- screen_models(refit_regular)
```

```{r copper-pareto-regular}
pareto_regular <- pareto_rows(screened_regular)
round_cols(pareto_regular)
```

```{r copper-weights-both}
copper_weights <- merge(weights_default,
                        weights_of(screened_regular, "regularizing"),
                        all = TRUE)
round_cols(copper_weights)
```

In this run the longer refit was applied to
`r name_list(retry_regular, "no equation")` under the regularising priors, and
the refit at a higher `adapt_delta` to
`r name_list(tune_regular, "no equation")`. After these refits the screen
retained `r length(screened_regular$success_models)` equations
(`r name_list(screened_regular$success_models)`), against
`r length(screened_default$success_models)` under the default priors. Under
the regularising priors `r sum(pareto_regular$n_high_k > 0)` of the retained
equations had at least one cell with *k* at or above 0.7, and as in step 3 the
weights of both sets are approximate. They sum to one within each set, and an
equation absent from a set is `NA`.

## The set used for inference

We use the regularising set for inference only if its screen retains more
equations than the screen of the default set, because only then do its stronger
assumptions make more of the candidate set usable. Otherwise the default set is
used for inference, and the regularising set provides the comparison of step 6.
The rule depends on the screens alone, so the choice of prior set does not
depend on the estimates.

```{r copper-final-set}
use_regular <- length(screened_regular$success_models) >
  length(screened_default$success_models)
screened_final <- if (use_regular) screened_regular else screened_default
final_prior <- if (use_regular) "regularizing" else "uninformative"
```

```{r copper-final-set-text, include = FALSE}
final_label <- if (use_regular) "regularising" else "default"
other_label <- if (use_regular) "default" else "regularising"
other_prior <- if (use_regular) "uninformative" else "regularizing"
```

The screen retained `r length(screened_default$success_models)` equations under
the default priors and `r length(screened_regular$success_models)` under the
regularising priors, so the analysis continues with the `r final_label` priors.

# 5. Fit diagnostics

Sampler diagnostics concern the posterior draws, whereas `check_fit()` compares
the observed and simulated response within each concentration. We apply it
first to the highest-weighted equation of the set chosen in step 4, and then to
each retained equation. The concentration with the censored reading
(`r signif(cens_conc, 2)` mg/L) is left out of the counts and ranges below,
because the reading at the floor is a bound and not a measurement.

## Spread by concentration

A constant `Gamma` `shape` implies a constant coefficient of variation. We use
`check_fit()` to compare the observed spread within each concentration with the
spread of replicate responses simulated from the highest-weighted equation.

```{r checkfit-const}
best_final <- pull_best(screened_final)
cf_const <- check_fit(best_final)
cf_const[, c("group", "n", "obs_sd", "sim_sd", "sd_ratio", "ppp_sd", "control")]
```

```{r checkfit-const-text, include = FALSE}
flagged_sd <- function(cf) {
  keep <- uncensored(cf$group)
  sum((cf$ppp_sd[keep] > 0.95 | cf$ppp_sd[keep] < 0.05), na.rm = TRUE)
}
keep_const <- uncensored(cf_const$group)
ratio_const <- cf_const$sd_ratio[keep_const]
group_const <- as.numeric(as.character(cf_const$group[keep_const]))
ctrl_const <- cf_const$sd_ratio[cf_const$control]
ctrl_text <- if (ctrl_const < 1) {
  paste0("At the control the fit simulates about ", round(1 / ctrl_const),
         " times the spread observed")
} else {
  paste0("At the control the fit simulates about ",
         sprintf("%.1f", 1 / ctrl_const), " of the spread observed")
}
both_ways <- ctrl_const < 1 && max(ratio_const) > 1
```

`sd_ratio` is the observed spread divided by the simulated spread, so a value of
1 indicates agreement. Over the concentrations without a censored reading it is
between `r sprintf("%.2f", min(ratio_const))` and
`r sprintf("%.2f", max(ratio_const))`, with its maximum at
`r signif(group_const[which.max(ratio_const)], 2)` mg/L, and
`r flagged_sd(cf_const)` of the `r sum(keep_const)` are flagged (a posterior
predictive p-value for the standard deviation outside [0.05, 0.95]).
`r ctrl_text`.
`r if (both_ways) paste0("Where the ratio is largest it simulates about ", round(max(ratio_const)), " times too little. A single shape cannot do both, and a constant coefficient of variation requires it to.") else ""`

The coefficient of variation computed from the four wells at one concentration
involves no curve, and cannot be inflated by any lack of fit in the mean. It
measures the same within-cell spread.

```{r copper-cv}
cv_obs <- tapply(cu$rlu_cens, cu$conc, function(z) sd(z) / mean(z))
round(cv_obs, 3)
```

```{r copper-cv-text, include = FALSE}
cv_keep <- cv_obs[uncensored(names(cv_obs))]
```

Over the concentrations without a censored reading, the coefficient of
variation is between `r sprintf("%.3f", min(cv_keep[1:5]))` and
`r sprintf("%.3f", max(cv_keep[1:5]))` over the five lowest, and its largest
value is `r sprintf("%.3f", max(cv_keep))` at
`r signif(as.numeric(names(cv_keep))[which.max(cv_keep)], 2)` mg/L, which is
`r round(max(cv_keep) / cv_keep[1])` times the control value. A `Gamma` fitted
with one `shape` returns a single value for all of them.

## The dispersion sub-model

`disp("power")` writes `log(shape)` as a linear function of `log(mu)`, so that
the dispersion parameter becomes a function of the fitted mean instead of a
constant. The variance functions available, the families to which each applies,
and what the estimated exponent implies for a given family are described in
[*Single model
usage*](https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example1.html).

We refit the candidate set with the sub-model under the prior set chosen in step
4 (the `r final_label` priors), so that the refit changes the dispersion
structure alone. A dispersion sub-model changes the posterior from which each
equation is sampled, so the refits and the screen of step 3 are applied again.
Because this is the set from which the estimates are reported, the cells with
Pareto *k* at or above 0.7 are also refitted exactly.

```{r disp-power, results = "hide"}
power_formula <- rlu_cens | cens(censoring) ~
  crf(log(conc), model = "decline") + ogl(conc_group) + disp("power")
set.seed(17)
fit_power <- bnec(power_formula, data = cu, family = gfam,
                  prior_type = final_prior, seed = 17, refresh = 0)
sampling_power <- check_sampling(fit_power)
retry_power <- retry_models(sampling_power)
tune_power <- tune_models(sampling_power)
set.seed(17)
refit_power <- refit_failed(fit_power, retry_power, tune_power)
sampling_power_refit <- check_sampling(refit_power)
```

```{r disp-power-screen, message = TRUE}
screened_power <- screen_models(refit_power)
pareto_power <- pareto_rows(screened_power)
round_cols(pareto_power)
```

```{r disp-power-reloo, results = "hide"}
refit_high_k <- function(x, p) {
  if (any(p$n_high_k > 0)) amend(x, loo_controls = list(refit = TRUE)) else x
}
set.seed(17)
screened_power <- refit_high_k(screened_power, pareto_power)
```

```{r disp-power-weights}
weights_power <- weights_of(screened_power, "power")
round_cols(weights_power)
best_power <- pull_best(screened_power)
```

In this run `r which_failed(fit_power)` failed to fit under the sub-model, the
longer refit was applied to `r name_list(retry_power, "no equation")`, and the
refit at a higher `adapt_delta` to `r name_list(tune_power, "no equation")`.
The screen then retained `r length(screened_power$success_models)` of the
`r nrow(sampling_power)` fitted equations, against
`r length(screened_final$success_models)` under constant dispersion, and
`r sum(pareto_power$n_high_k > 0)` of the retained equations had a cell with *k*
at or above 0.7 before the exact refits. With the exact scores,
`r name_list(best_power$model)` has the highest weight
(`r sprintf("%.2f", max(weights_power$power))`).

```{r disp-slope}
brms::fixef(pull_brmsfit(best_power))[c("c0_Intercept", "c1_Intercept"), ]
```

`c1` is the exponent, and a value of zero corresponds to the constant-dispersion
model. The default prior bounds `c1` to the range 0 to 2 for a `Gamma` response,
so an interval well inside that range reflects the data and not the bound. The
coefficients of variation computed above provide an estimate of this exponent
that involves no fit, because a power law makes `log(cv)` linear in `log(mu)`,
with slope `-c1/2`.

```{r disp-slope-observed}
conc_mean <- tapply(cu$rlu_cens, cu$conc, mean)
keep_cv <- uncensored(names(cv_obs))
obs_slope <- lm(log(cv_obs[keep_cv]) ~ log(conc_mean[keep_cv]))
# Multiplying by -2 reverses the order of the bounds, so the names confint()
# supplied would be attached to the wrong ends.
setNames(-2 * c(coef(obs_slope)[[2]], rev(confint(obs_slope)[2, ])),
         c("c1", "lower", "upper"))
c(r_squared = summary(obs_slope)$r.squared)
```

```{r disp-slope-text, include = FALSE}
c1_fit <- brms::fixef(pull_brmsfit(best_power))["c1_Intercept", ]
c1_wells <- -2 * c(coef(obs_slope)[[2]], rev(confint(obs_slope)[2, ]))
c1_overlap <- c1_fit[["Q2.5"]] <= c1_wells[3] && c1_wells[2] <= c1_fit[["Q97.5"]]
```

The fit gives `r sprintf("%.2f", c1_fit[["Estimate"]])`
(`r sprintf("%.2f", c1_fit[["Q2.5"]])` to `r sprintf("%.2f", c1_fit[["Q97.5"]])`)
for `r name_list(best_power$model)`, and the wells give
`r sprintf("%.2f", c1_wells[1])` (`r sprintf("%.2f", c1_wells[2])` to
`r sprintf("%.2f", c1_wells[3])`), so the two intervals
`r if (c1_overlap) "overlap" else "do not overlap"`.
`r if (c1_overlap) "This agreement indicates that the sub-model describes the replicate variation the design measured, and does not absorb a failure of the curve elsewhere in the series." else "The sub-model therefore describes a relationship between spread and mean that the wells alone do not show, and the curve should be examined before the sub-model is accepted."`
A within-concentration standard deviation involves no curve, which is what makes
that comparison informative. Whether a power law is the right form for the
relationship is a separate question, which the regression addresses with an
R-squared of `r sprintf("%.3f", summary(obs_slope)$r.squared)` on
`r summary(obs_slope)$df[2]` degrees of freedom.

## Checking the dispersion sub-model

We repeat the within-concentration check after fitting `disp("power")`.

```{r checkfit-power}
cf_power <- check_fit(best_power)
cf_power[, c("group", "n", "obs_sd", "sim_sd", "sd_ratio", "ppp_sd", "control")]
```

For the highest-weighted equation, the number of concentrations without a
censored reading that are flagged for spread changes from
`r flagged_sd(cf_const)` to `r flagged_sd(cf_power)`, and the control ratio
changes from `r sprintf("%.2f", cf_const$sd_ratio[cf_const$control])` to
`r sprintf("%.2f", cf_power$sd_ratio[cf_power$control])`. Because the
estimates use model averages, we also check each retained equation:

```{r checkfit-model-sets}
cf_const_set <- check_fit(screened_final)
cf_power_set <- check_fit(screened_power)
fit_scale_summary <- function(x, label) {
  parts <- split(as.data.frame(x), x$model)
  out <- do.call(rbind, lapply(parts, function(d) {
    data.frame(model = d$model[1], wi = d$wi[1],
               ctrl_ratio = d$sd_ratio[d$control], n_flag = flagged_sd(d))
  }))
  names(out)[-1] <- paste(names(out)[-1], label, sep = "_")
  out
}
scale_summary <- merge(fit_scale_summary(cf_const_set, "const"),
                       fit_scale_summary(cf_power_set, "power"), all = TRUE)
round_cols(scale_summary)
```

The table gives, for each equation under each structure, its weight (`wi`), the
ratio of observed to simulated standard deviation at the control (`ctrl_ratio`),
and the number of concentrations without a censored reading that are flagged
for spread (`n_flag`), with `NA` where the screen removed the equation. Under
constant dispersion the control ratio is below 0.5 in
`r sum(scale_summary$ctrl_ratio_const < 0.5, na.rm = TRUE)` of the
`r sum(!is.na(scale_summary$ctrl_ratio_const))` retained equations, and under
the sub-model in `r sum(scale_summary$ctrl_ratio_power < 0.5, na.rm = TRUE)` of
`r sum(!is.na(scale_summary$ctrl_ratio_power))`. These counts show how far the
discrepancy found in the highest-weighted equation is shared by the rest of each
set.

`plot()` draws each statistic against the spread that the fit simulates for it,
and takes both fits at once where a `model` column distinguishes the panels.
Because the response falls from about
`r format(signif(max(conc_means), 3), big.mark = ",", scientific = FALSE)`
relative light units at the control to about
`r format(signif(min(conc_means), 3), big.mark = ",", scientific = FALSE)` at
its lowest, we put the display on a logarithmic scale, as on a linear scale the
lower half of the series would be a row of points on the axis.

```{r exmp9-disp-checkfit, fig.asp = 0.82, out.width = "92%"}
both <- rbind(cbind(cf_const, model = "constant"),
              cbind(cf_power, model = "disp(\"power\")"))
class(both) <- class(cf_const)
plot(both) +
  scale_y_log10(labels = function(x) {
    format(x, big.mark = ",", scientific = FALSE)
  })
```

```{r disp-checkfit-text, include = FALSE}
inside <- function(cf, stat) {
  keep <- uncensored(cf$group)
  lo <- cf[[paste0("sim_", stat, "_lo")]][keep]
  hi <- cf[[paste0("sim_", stat, "_hi")]][keep]
  obs <- cf[[paste0("obs_", stat)]][keep]
  sum(obs >= lo & obs <= hi)
}
```

Each bar is the 95% span of the statistic simulated from the fit, and each point
is the observed value. Of the `r sum(keep_const)` concentrations without a
censored reading, the observed mean falls inside its bar at
`r inside(cf_const, "mean")` under constant dispersion and at
`r inside(cf_power, "mean")` under the sub-model. This says little about the
curve, because `check_fit()` simulates each concentration with its fitted cell
deviation, which can absorb a departure of the mean from the curve. The spread
of the four wells within a cell is unaffected by that deviation, so the scale
panels remain a test of the dispersion structure. The observed standard
deviation falls inside its bar at `r inside(cf_const, "sd")` of those
concentrations under constant dispersion and at `r inside(cf_power, "sd")`
under the sub-model, and at the control it is
`r format(signif(cf_const$obs_sd[cf_const$control], 3), big.mark = ",", scientific = FALSE)`
against a simulated span of
`r format(signif(cf_const$sim_sd_lo[cf_const$control], 3), big.mark = ",", scientific = FALSE)`
to
`r format(signif(cf_const$sim_sd_hi[cf_const$control], 3), big.mark = ",", scientific = FALSE)`
under constant dispersion, and
`r format(signif(cf_power$sim_sd_lo[cf_power$control], 3), big.mark = ",", scientific = FALSE)`
to
`r format(signif(cf_power$sim_sd_hi[cf_power$control], 3), big.mark = ",", scientific = FALSE)`
under the sub-model. A model can reproduce the mean of each group and still be
wrong about the spread, which is the case described in the Overview.

# 6. Prior sensitivity and reporting

## The influence of the priors

The prior set was chosen in step 4 on the constant-dispersion fits. To show how
much the priors change the reported estimates, we fit the dispersion sub-model
under the `r other_label` priors as well, and compare the estimates from the
smooth (ECx) equations that pass the screen under both prior sets, excluding
`ecxflat`. The comparison needs only the equations that pass under both, so the
equations that fail under the `r other_label` priors are not refitted. The
weights of each common set are recomputed without exact refits, so they may be
approximate (step 3), and both sides of the comparison are computed in the same
way.

```{r copper-power-other, results = "hide"}
set.seed(17)
fit_power_other <- bnec(power_formula, data = cu, family = gfam,
                        prior_type = other_prior, seed = 17, refresh = 0)
screened_power_other <- screen_models(fit_power_other)
```

```{r copper-common-set, results = "hide"}
power_sets <- setNames(list(screened_power, screened_power_other),
                       c(final_prior, other_prior))
shared_models <- intersect(names(models(screened_power)),
                           names(models(screened_power_other)))
shared_models <- setdiff(shared_models[grepl("^ecx", shared_models)], "ecxflat")
if (!length(shared_models)) {
  stop("No smooth equation passed the screen under both priors.")
}
make_common <- function(x, selected) {
  fits <- lapply(selected, function(m) pull_out(x, model = m))
  if (length(fits) == 1L) fits[[1]] else do.call(c, fits)
}
common_default <- make_common(power_sets[["uninformative"]], shared_models)
common_regular <- make_common(power_sets[["regularizing"]], shared_models)
```

```{r copper-prior-effect}
estimate_rows <- function(x, label) {
  est <- rbind(EC10 = ecx(x, ecx_val = 10), EC50 = ecx(x, ecx_val = 50),
               NSEC = nsec(x))[, c("Q50", "Q2.5", "Q97.5")]
  data.frame(estimate = rownames(est), prior_type = label, est,
             row.names = NULL)
}
prior_effect <- rbind(estimate_rows(common_default, "uninformative"),
                      estimate_rows(common_regular, "regularizing"))
prior_effect <- prior_effect[order(prior_effect$estimate), ]
round_cols(prior_effect)
```

```{r copper-prior-effect-values, include = FALSE}
pe <- function(est, prior, col = "Q50") {
  prior_effect[prior_effect$estimate == est &
                 prior_effect$prior_type == prior, col]
}
interval_width <- function(est, prior) pe(est, prior, "Q97.5") - pe(est, prior, "Q2.5")
```

Each pair of rows estimates one quantity from the common set
(`r name_list(shared_models)`), fitted to identical observations, so the
difference between rows can be attributed to the priors within those equations.
The median NSEC is `r sprintf("%.3f", pe("NSEC", "uninformative"))` mg/L under
the default priors and `r sprintf("%.3f", pe("NSEC", "regularizing"))` mg/L
under the regularising priors, and the median EC50 is
`r sprintf("%.3f", pe("EC50", "uninformative"))` and
`r sprintf("%.3f", pe("EC50", "regularizing"))` mg/L. The width of the NSEC
interval is `r sprintf("%.3f", interval_width("NSEC", "uninformative"))` and
`r sprintf("%.3f", interval_width("NSEC", "regularizing"))` mg/L. Where an
interval is narrower under the regularising priors, the difference may reflect
their stronger assumptions as well as information from the data, and cannot be
presented as precision supplied solely by the experiment.

The set used for inference can contain equations that are not in the common set
(e.g., `ecxflat`, which the comparison excludes), so its model-averaged
estimates also reflect their weights and cannot be interpreted as a pure effect
of the priors.
[*Priors*](https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example3.html)
describes how the priors are constructed and how to compare their densities with
the posterior.

## The final model-averaged fit

We report the estimates of the screened set fitted with the dispersion sub-model
under the `r final_label` priors (step 5), shown here with its credible band.

```{r exmp9-autoplot, fig.asp = 0.72, out.width = "80%"}
autoplot(screened_power) + copper_axis
```

The curve is the model-averaged prediction over the retained equations, and the
band is its 95% credible interval, both returned on the recorded concentration
scale although the formula fitted `log(conc)`. The vertical lines mark the
model-averaged *N(S)EC* and its bounds. The table gives the median and
equal-tailed 95% credible interval of the EC10, EC50 and NSEC (mg/L) from this
set. Here `nsec()` applies one control-reference definition to each retained
equation, whereas, where a set mixes threshold and smooth equations, `nec()`
returns the model-averaged mixture of NEC and NSEC draws that `summary()` labels
N(S)EC.

```{r copper-final-estimates, message = TRUE, warning = TRUE}
copper_estimates <- rbind(
  EC10 = ecx(screened_power, ecx_val = 10),
  EC50 = ecx(screened_power, ecx_val = 50),
  NSEC = nsec(screened_power)
)[, c("Q50", "Q2.5", "Q97.5")]
round(copper_estimates, 4)
```

The model-averaged EC10 is `r sprintf("%.3f", copper_estimates["EC10", "Q50"])`
mg/L (`r sprintf("%.3f", copper_estimates["EC10", "Q2.5"])` to
`r sprintf("%.3f", copper_estimates["EC10", "Q97.5"])`), the EC50 is
`r sprintf("%.3f", copper_estimates["EC50", "Q50"])` mg/L
(`r sprintf("%.3f", copper_estimates["EC50", "Q2.5"])` to
`r sprintf("%.3f", copper_estimates["EC50", "Q97.5"])`), and the NSEC is
`r sprintf("%.3f", copper_estimates["NSEC", "Q50"])` mg/L
(`r sprintf("%.3f", copper_estimates["NSEC", "Q2.5"])` to
`r sprintf("%.3f", copper_estimates["NSEC", "Q97.5"])`). The ECx values use the
default absolute response scale, and `type = "range"` would instead measure the
decline between the fitted upper and lower response levels. A flat equation may
have no ECx crossing within the tested range, in which case the messages and
warnings above report how many draws were censored.

## The dispersion structure and the estimates

The constant-dispersion set of step 4 and the sub-model describe a common mean,
and differ in how wide each makes the posterior at the control. The weights of
the constant-dispersion set are approximate (step 3), which is sufficient for
this comparison.

```{r disp-estimates}
dispersion_estimates <- rbind(
  constant_NSEC = nsec(screened_final),
  power_NSEC = nsec(screened_power),
  constant_EC10 = ecx(screened_final, ecx_val = 10),
  power_EC10 = ecx(screened_power, ecx_val = 10)
)[, c("Q50", "Q2.5", "Q97.5")]
round(dispersion_estimates, 4)
```

```{r control-posterior}
control_reference <- function(fit) {
  nd <- data.frame(conc = min(cu$conc), censoring = "none")
  post <- posterior_epred(pull_brmsfit(fit), newdata = nd, re_formula = NA)[, 1]
  c(sd = sd(post), cv = sd(post) / mean(post),
    reference_share = unname(quantile(post, 0.01) / mean(post)))
}
control_posterior <- rbind(constant = control_reference(best_final),
                           power = control_reference(best_power))
round(control_posterior, 4)
```

```{r control-posterior-text, include = FALSE}
nsec_const <- dispersion_estimates["constant_NSEC", "Q50"]
nsec_power <- dispersion_estimates["power_NSEC", "Q50"]
sd_factor <- control_posterior["constant", "sd"] / control_posterior["power", "sd"]
direction_text <- if (sd_factor > 1 && nsec_const > nsec_power) {
  paste("A reference placed lower is crossed further along the curve, so the",
        "constant-dispersion fit returns the *less* protective figure, and",
        "nothing in its sampler diagnostics indicates this.")
} else {
  paste("The sampler diagnostics do not distinguish between the two figures;",
        "the check of spread in step 5 does.")
}
```

The median *NSEC* is `r sprintf("%.3f", nsec_const)` mg/L under constant
dispersion and `r sprintf("%.3f", nsec_power)` mg/L under the sub-model. The
change arises through the quantity from which `nsec()` reads its reference,
which is the posterior of the control mean with any group-level deviation
excluded. For the highest-weighted equations, this posterior is
`r sprintf("%.1f", sd_factor)` times as wide under constant dispersion as under
the sub-model. Because `nsec()` sets its reference at a lower quantile of it,
the reference is at
`r sprintf("%.2f", control_posterior["constant", "reference_share"])` of the
control mean under constant dispersion, against
`r sprintf("%.2f", control_posterior["power", "reference_share"])` under the
sub-model. `r direction_text`

The EC10 is `r sprintf("%.3f", dispersion_estimates["constant_EC10", "Q50"])`
mg/L under constant dispersion and
`r sprintf("%.3f", dispersion_estimates["power_EC10", "Q50"])` mg/L under the
sub-model, with interval widths of
`r sprintf("%.3f", diff(dispersion_estimates["constant_EC10", c("Q2.5", "Q97.5")]))`
and
`r sprintf("%.3f", diff(dispersion_estimates["power_EC10", c("Q2.5", "Q97.5")]))`
mg/L. Because the EC10 is measured at a fixed fraction of the control mean, and
not at a quantile of its posterior, the variance assumption affects it through
the precision of the curve, and not through the placement of its reference. This
is the difference between the *NSEC* and the ECx described in the Overview.

These values depend on the treatment of the floor reading as left-censored at
the smallest positive reading on the plate, on the cell term, on
`asymptote_observed = TRUE`, on the candidate set, on the `r final_label`
priors and on the dispersion sub-model, and they describe one plate. A methods
section should state each of these.

# Draft methods and results text

Here we give a draft of how the analysis above might be described in a paper.
It refers to steps 1 to 6 of this vignette as Supplementary Material S1 to S6
(e.g., the sampler diagnostics table of step 3 would be Table S3). The values
are those computed above, and would update with any change to the analysis.

## Statistical analysis

```{r copper-methods-text, include = FALSE}
regular_reason <- if (length(still_failed) || length(unfit_default)) {
  "Because equations still failed after these refits, the"
} else {
  "The"
}
spread_reason <- if (flagged_sd(cf_const) > 0) {
  paste0("Because the observed standard deviation lay outside the range ",
         "simulated by the highest-weighted equation at ", flagged_sd(cf_const),
         " concentrations, the")
} else {
  "The"
}
```

Concentrations of copper that reduced the luminescence of the Lum-31 assay by
10% and 50% (EC10 and EC50), and the no-significant-effect concentration (NSEC;
@fisherfox2023), were estimated for one plate read at 15 minutes using the R
package `bayesnec` [@fisher2024], which fits concentration-response models in
Stan through `brms` [@Burkner2017]. Luminescence (relative light units) was
modelled as a function of log measured copper concentration, using a gamma
distribution with an identity link and a group-level intercept for the four
wells at each concentration. Readings at or below the blank were treated as
left-censored at the smallest positive reading on the plate (S1). The candidate
model set comprised the `r length(prior_default)` equations of the `bayesnec`
`decline` group that are valid for these data (declining equations and one
constant equation; S2), fitted first with the package's default (uninformative)
priors. Each equation was fitted with four chains of 10,000 iterations, of which
the first 8,000 were discarded as warmup. Equations were excluded where the
maximum R-hat exceeded 1.01 or the minimum bulk or tail effective sample size
was below 400 [@Vehtari2021], or where more than ten divergent transitions
occurred; an equation that failed only on R-hat or effective sample size was
first refitted with twice the number of iterations, and one with more than ten
divergent transitions with a target acceptance rate (`adapt_delta`) of 0.99, or
0.999 where its first fit had already used 0.99 (S3). `r regular_reason`
candidate set was refitted with the package's regularising priors, which are
narrower and centre the upper and lower response levels on the mean response at
the lowest and highest concentrations, and screened in the same way. The set
fitted with the regularising priors was to be used for inference only if more of
its equations passed the screen (S4). The fit of each retained equation was
assessed by comparing the observed mean and standard deviation of the response
at each concentration with those simulated from the posterior.
`r spread_reason` candidate set was refitted under the chosen priors with the
logarithm of the gamma shape parameter modelled as a linear function of the
logarithm of the mean (a power-law dispersion sub-model), and refitted and
screened as above (S5). Posterior predictions from the retained equations of
this set were combined using pseudo-Bayesian model averaging weights [@Yao2018]
calculated from cross-validation that left out the four wells of one
concentration at a time [@vehtari2017], with exact refits where the Pareto *k*
diagnostic was at or above 0.7 (S5). The sensitivity of the estimates to the
priors was assessed by fitting the dispersion sub-model with the other prior
set, and comparing the estimates from the smooth (ECx) equations that passed the
screen under both (S6). Both prior sets bounded the NEC and EC50 priors to the
tested concentration range (the package default).

## Results

```{r copper-results-text, include = FALSE}
kept_default <- screened_default$success_models
kept_regular <- screened_regular$success_models
screen_text <- paste0(
  "Under the default priors the sampler screen retained ", length(kept_default),
  " of the ", length(valid), " candidate equations (", name_list(kept_default),
  "; S3)",
  if (length(unfit_default)) {
    paste0(", and ", name_list(unfit_default), " failed to fit")
  }, "."
)
regular_text <- paste0(
  " Under the regularising priors ",
  if (length(rescued)) paste0(name_list(rescued), " also fitted and "),
  "the screen retained ", length(kept_regular), " equations",
  if (length(intersect(rescued, kept_regular))) {
    paste0(", including ", name_list(intersect(rescued, kept_regular)))
  },
  "; the first run took ", total_minutes(seconds_regular),
  " min of warmup and sampling, against ", total_minutes(seconds_default),
  " min under the default priors (S4)."
)
final_text <- paste0(" The analysis therefore used the ", final_label,
                     " priors.")
spread_text <- paste0(
  "Under constant dispersion the highest-weighted equation ",
  if (ctrl_const < 1) {
    paste0("simulated about ", round(1 / ctrl_const),
           " times the standard deviation observed at the control")
  } else {
    paste0("simulated ", sprintf("%.2f", 1 / ctrl_const),
           " of the standard deviation observed at the control")
  },
  ", and the observed standard deviation lay outside its simulated range at ",
  flagged_sd(cf_const), " of ", sum(keep_const), " concentrations (S5)."
)
power_text <- paste0(
  " With the dispersion sub-model the estimated exponent was ",
  sprintf("%.2f", c1_fit[["Estimate"]]), " (95% CI ",
  sprintf("%.2f", c1_fit[["Q2.5"]]), "–", sprintf("%.2f", c1_fit[["Q97.5"]]),
  "), against ", sprintf("%.2f", c1_wells[1]), " (",
  sprintf("%.2f", c1_wells[2]), "–", sprintf("%.2f", c1_wells[3]),
  ") estimated from the wells without a fitted curve, ",
  flagged_sd(cf_power), " concentrations were flagged, and the screen retained ",
  length(screened_power$success_models), " equations, of which ",
  name_list(best_power$model), " had the highest weight (",
  sprintf("%.2f", max(weights_power$power)), "; S5)."
)
```

Mean luminescence fell from
`r format(signif(conc_means[1], 3), big.mark = ",", scientific = FALSE)`
relative light units in the controls to below one per cent of that value from
`r signif(conc_levels[first_low], 2)` mg/L, and the coefficient of variation
among the four wells at a concentration rose from
`r sprintf("%.3f", cv_keep[1])` at the control to a maximum of
`r sprintf("%.3f", max(cv_keep))` as the response fell (S1).
`r screen_text``r regular_text``r final_text`

`r spread_text``r power_text` The modelled concentrations that reduced
luminescence by 10% and 50% (EC10 and EC50) were
`r sprintf("%.3f", copper_estimates["EC10", "Q50"])` (95% CI
`r sprintf("%.3f", copper_estimates["EC10", "Q2.5"])`–`r sprintf("%.3f", copper_estimates["EC10", "Q97.5"])`)
and `r sprintf("%.3f", copper_estimates["EC50", "Q50"])`
(`r sprintf("%.3f", copper_estimates["EC50", "Q2.5"])`–`r sprintf("%.3f", copper_estimates["EC50", "Q97.5"])`)
mg/L, respectively, and the NSEC was
`r sprintf("%.3f", copper_estimates["NSEC", "Q50"])`
(`r sprintf("%.3f", copper_estimates["NSEC", "Q2.5"])`–`r sprintf("%.3f", copper_estimates["NSEC", "Q97.5"])`)
mg/L (Table S6). Under constant dispersion the NSEC was
`r sprintf("%.3f", nsec_const)` mg/L. For the smooth equations that passed under
both prior sets (`r name_list(shared_models)`), the median NSEC was
`r sprintf("%.3f", pe("NSEC", "uninformative"))` mg/L under the default priors
and `r sprintf("%.3f", pe("NSEC", "regularizing"))` mg/L under the regularising
priors (S6).

# Summary

```{r copper-summary-counts, include = FALSE}
removed <- function(tab) sum(tab$failed)
```

The sampler screen was applied to three fits of the same candidate set. After
the refits of step 3 it removed `r removed(sampling_default_refit)` of the
`r nrow(sampling_default_refit)` fitted equations under the default priors,
after the same refits `r removed(sampling_regular_refit)` of
`r nrow(sampling_regular_refit)` under the regularising priors, and
`r removed(sampling_power_refit)` of `r nrow(sampling_power_refit)` with the
dispersion sub-model. Which equations fail cannot be predicted before the screen
is run, so the equations removed, and the reasons, should be reported for each
fit; a prior choice that reduces failures in one equation may increase them in
another.

The diagnostic table separates inadequate effective sample size from divergent
transitions, and both from a failure to fit. A longer run may resolve an
ESS-only failure, whereas divergences call for examining posterior geometry,
priors and parameterisation. An equation that does not fit has no diagnostics,
so none of the refits of step 3 applies to it, and a change of priors is the
remedy available. `r if (length(rescued)) paste0("On this plate ", name_list(rescued), " failed to fit under the default priors and fitted under the regularising priors.") else ""`
Excluding an equation at the stated cutoff is an analysis decision that should
be reported.

The variance assumption is the one that no sampler diagnostic detects. On this
plate the coefficient of variation among the wells rose from
`r sprintf("%.3f", cv_keep[1])` at the control to
`r sprintf("%.3f", max(cv_keep))`, and the constant-dispersion set passed the
sampler screen for most of its equations while its highest-weighted equation
simulated a control spread `r sprintf("%.2f", 1 / ctrl_const)` times the one
observed. Adding `disp("power")` changed the number of concentrations flagged
for spread in that equation from `r flagged_sd(cf_const)` to
`r flagged_sd(cf_power)`, and the model-averaged *NSEC* from
`r sprintf("%.3f", nsec_const)` to `r sprintf("%.3f", nsec_power)` mg/L. A fit
can therefore pass the checks the sampler screen applies and still simulate the
wrong spread at the concentration from which both estimators are referenced.

Several decisions made around the fits also determine the estimates: the family
was chosen on the support of the response, the floor reading was treated as
left-censored at a bound inferred from the plate, one plate was analysed so that
no term for the gain of the plate reader was needed, the cell term set the unit
of replication, the prior set was chosen by a rule fixed before the estimates
were read, and `asymptote_observed = TRUE` was kept. None of these is a result
the workflow produces automatically, and each is a decision that the analysis
needs to record.

# References
